WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.234
Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-nominated,

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:05.534
Emmy, Golden Globe,
and Tony-winning actor

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:07.234
you know from shows
like "Breaking Bad"

00:00:07.234 --> 00:00:09.734
and "Malcolm in the Middle"
and films like "Trumbo."

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:11.167
He now stars in "Your Honor."

00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:14.267
The second season finale
premieres March 17th

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:15.501
on streaming and On Demand

00:00:15.501 --> 00:00:18.267
and airs March 19th on Showtime.
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:22.300
-I held my son in my arms
as he died.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:26.300
♪♪

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.801
-And did you see who shot him?

00:00:29.801 --> 00:00:32.000
-Yes.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.767
-And was it the defendant,
Eugene Jones?

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:41.067
♪♪

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:43.300
-Yes, it was.
-Please welcome back

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.400
to the show our friend
Bryan Cranston, everybody.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:54.834
♪♪

00:00:54.834 --> 00:01:02.234
♪♪

00:01:02.234 --> 00:01:04.601
Welcome back!
-Thank you. Good to be back.

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:06.767
It's been four years.
-It's been four years.

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.701
I'm very, very happy
to have you here.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:11.234
-Well, your statute
of limitations was up.

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:12.367
-Yeah.

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:14.367
-And the restraining order
went away.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:16.567
-And I can't thank you enough
for giving me another chance.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:18.234
-I -- Well, we'll give it
one more chance.

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:19.801
-And what you are doing
after the show?

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:23.133
-God! Here it goes again, Seth!
Back off!

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:24.534
-Hey, I wanted to ask.

00:01:24.534 --> 00:01:26.934
So, this is a wonderful show
in its second season.

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:29.601
And a very intense show.
You play a --

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:31.200
-How can you tell?

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.133
-You play a former judge
in new Orleans. This I like.

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:34.801
This poster lets you know
what you're in for.

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:36.434
You're a guy who's
going through some stuff.

00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:37.868
-He's going through some things,
yeah.

00:01:37.868 --> 00:01:40.767
-Obviously this is a real beard.
You did not --

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.534
-That is my real beard, yeah.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:45.300
-It was a little shorter
in the clip we saw.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.100
So, you trimmed your beard down
over the course of the season.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.968
Did you enjoy walking around
with a big old bush like this?

00:01:49.968 --> 00:01:52.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:55.801
-You see, that's what brought
about the restraining order,

00:01:55.801 --> 00:01:57.567
comments like that.
-It's a good word.

00:01:57.567 --> 00:01:59.734
It's a good word
for what you're showing me here.

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:02.300
-It is, it is. It's appropriate
and suggestive.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.801
[ Laughter ]
You know, I did because --

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:08.400
It almost created
a mask-like effect.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.534
-Yeah, I would get that.
-And it's nice to be able

00:02:10.534 --> 00:02:12.834
to walk around
and not be so noticed

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:14.267
and be able to observe,

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.534
which is, you know,
part of the actor's tool chest.

00:02:16.534 --> 00:02:18.300
-Which gets a little bit harder
to do

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.834
once you become recognizable.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:19.834 --> 00:02:21.801
-Now, you've cut it down

00:02:21.801 --> 00:02:25.701
to a very substantive mustache
right now.

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:27.934
-It is --
Yeah, it's a little dip.

00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:30.667
I'm doing a movie
with Allison Janney.

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.701
It takes place in the '80s.
-Okay.

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:35.801
-And I always think that
someone sets their look

00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:39.000
in the period that they were
really the most formative.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.467
So I think my character might
have set his look in the '70s,

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:46.067
with what could be construed
as a porn 'stache.

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:48.300
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. I think so.
-Is it? Is it?

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.834
And then so I'm not quite sure.

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:55.033
I want to ask your audience
if they think this look

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.234
with the little, the little --
What do they call it?

00:02:57.234 --> 00:02:59.334
-Like a soul patch.
-Soul patch here.

00:02:59.334 --> 00:03:01.968
-Do you think
that looks nastier?

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:04.467
Or does -- Without it?

00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:06.367
-Oh, that's
an interesting thing.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:08.767
Alright.
I think with it, it's nastier.

00:03:08.767 --> 00:03:09.834
-With it?
-Yeah.

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:11.934
-It's nastier?
-I would say, with it,

00:03:11.934 --> 00:03:14.834
I don't want that guy
to be, like, my pilot.

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:16.834
-Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:03:16.834 --> 00:03:19.434
-Okay. So I can do this?

00:03:19.434 --> 00:03:21.434
Or else just have my finger here
the whole time.

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:23.634
-Yeah. I think that's
probably the best solution.

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:25.400
-"I see what you mean."

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.501
-Because now you're coming off
as a very thoughtful guy.

00:03:27.501 --> 00:03:28.634
-"Ahh!"

00:03:28.634 --> 00:03:29.901
-And then you take it away,
though.

00:03:29.901 --> 00:03:31.200
You can't take it away,
or it's like,

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.601
"Oh, I've been talking
to a porn actor!"

00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:34.868
[ Laughter ]
"Taking advice from this guy!"

00:03:34.868 --> 00:03:36.467
-That's it.
"What am I doing?!"

00:03:36.467 --> 00:03:38.300
What I learned
on "Breaking Bad" --

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.834
Because when I started
that show...

00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:40.968
-I'm glad you brought it up.

00:03:40.968 --> 00:03:42.133
Because we have
some photos here.

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:43.501
-Oh. There you go.
-'Cause this is great.

00:03:43.501 --> 00:03:44.501
I mean, great mustache.

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.567
The mustache
to inform the character.

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:49.167
-You can do an entire episode
just on facial hair.

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:50.467
-Yeah.
-I really think you could.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:52.434
-I would love to. I mean,
would you be my first guest?

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:53.801
-I will be --
Now, have you --

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:55.701
Have you worn any --
-I can't do it.

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:58.100
I really -- I've tried.
I would love to grow a mustache.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.000
It would take me from now
until -- I'd be on a deathbed.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.167
It just --
It comes in very patchy.

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.634
-Well, see,
I would love to see that.

00:04:04.634 --> 00:04:05.934
-My dad -- You can see it.

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:08.334
Because my dad has a mustache,
and it looks exactly

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:09.968
-Right.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.067
-Well, when I was doing
"Breaking Bad,"

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:15.334
I wanted to have my mustache
as Walter White

00:04:15.334 --> 00:04:18.434
before his transition
into Heisenberg.

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:20.434
I wanted it to look --

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:22.367
For lack of a better word,
I said "impotent."

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:24.334
-Yeah.
-I want an impotent mustache.

00:04:24.334 --> 00:04:26.434
-It's a pretty --
It's a pretty impotent mustache.

00:04:26.434 --> 00:04:28.834
-Yeah.
-Like, it is weird.

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:30.801
I mean, there's a little
Ned Flanders to it.

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:33.167
-A little Ned Flanders.
"Howdiddy diddily do, neighbor?"

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:34.901
-Yeah.

00:04:34.901 --> 00:04:36.868
-There is a little Ned Flanders
to him, yes.

00:04:36.868 --> 00:04:40.133
And what I realized is
there's a few things.

00:04:40.133 --> 00:04:43.567
You can't have it too dark.
So I lightened it up.

00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:47.200
Also, you can never have
the hair

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.734
curl down below
the crease of the lips.

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:51.834
-Uh-huh.
-If -- Once it -- You can see.

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:53.000
-Yeah.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.334
-If I had a mustache
straight across...

00:04:55.334 --> 00:04:57.300
Here I am holding my --
-Do you need a --

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.567
-Yeah. Yeah, there you go.

00:04:59.567 --> 00:05:01.634
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.300
There you go. See?

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.467
You -- You came up with
a three-fingered cover.

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:09.300
-It's like --
-Never grow a mustache

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.701
that needs a three-finger cover!
-Got to have it!

00:05:11.701 --> 00:05:13.267
-And then -- But this is
the transformation.

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:15.234
I mean, look at that.
That is -- I mean --

00:05:15.234 --> 00:05:17.601
It's shocking
that it's the same actor.

00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:19.200
I mean, it really is amazing.
-Yeah.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.901
And so we wanted to really
make it look different.

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:24.467
And so I grew the --
This is actually not --

00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:27.501
If I just had this,
that's a goatee.

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:31.133
But goatee with the mustache
is called a Van Dyke.

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:34.234
-Look at you. You're such a --
You're so informed about this.

00:05:34.234 --> 00:05:35.901
But can I tell you --

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:37.868
can I tell you my favorite
of your mustaches?

00:05:37.868 --> 00:05:39.267
-Yes.
-Cause this is -- this is

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:41.901
a photo that is beloved
in my home.

00:05:41.901 --> 00:05:44.834
-Why?
-Because -- 2014 Emmys.

00:05:44.834 --> 00:05:46.601
There was an Emmy party.

00:05:46.601 --> 00:05:49.400
And my wife, my sister-in-law,
my brother's girlfriend,

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.234
my cousin,
they took a photo with you.

00:05:51.234 --> 00:05:54.067
And I forgot your mustache then.

00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:56.133
And this was a night where you
brought home a lot of Emmy gold.

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:58.033
Look at that one.

00:05:58.033 --> 00:05:59.901
I mean,
that's a real fun mustache.

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:02.968
-It's kind of an Errol Flynn.
-Did you -- Was that for a role?

00:06:02.968 --> 00:06:04.834
Or was that just for --

00:06:04.834 --> 00:06:07.200
-I was seeing --
I was about to do "Trumbo."

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.234
-There you go.
That's what I thought.

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:11.634
-I was about to do...
and "Trumbo" movie.

00:06:11.634 --> 00:06:14.534
And so I wanted to see
if that mustache --

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:15.934
We ended up not using it.

00:06:15.934 --> 00:06:18.434
I think I put
a fake mustache on.

00:06:18.434 --> 00:06:19.901
I can't remember.
-Yeah.

00:06:19.901 --> 00:06:21.434
-It's too long ago.
-You've had so many mustaches.

00:06:21.434 --> 00:06:24.701
-Lots of mustaches.
I do have fun with facial hair.

00:06:24.701 --> 00:06:26.267
-You do.
-I do.

00:06:26.267 --> 00:06:27.667
-And it's fun to watch.
-Yeah.

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:30.033
-I have no complaints about
any of the choices you made.

00:06:30.033 --> 00:06:32.901
We had your wonderful co-star.
Rosie Perez was here.

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:35.267
And she told about --
She wasn't in season one.

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:37.267
She was in season two
of "Your Honor."

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:40.067
And you basically --
you called her on a Friday

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:42.200
for her to come to work
on Monday.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.667
And I would imagine,
you know, with any actor.

00:06:44.667 --> 00:06:47.300
you have to be pretty convincing
to ask them to get on a plane

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.801
with, you know,
not a lot of time to prepare.

00:06:49.801 --> 00:06:52.701
-We needed someone formidable,

00:06:52.701 --> 00:06:58.234
someone who can -- is known
to have guts and strong.

00:06:58.234 --> 00:07:00.968
Right? And her name came up.

00:07:00.968 --> 00:07:03.367
I went, "Oh, my God. That's it.
Rosie Perez.

00:07:03.367 --> 00:07:05.400
We've got to get Rosie Perez."

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.100
And they said,
"We'll never get her."

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:08.367
She lives in New York.

00:07:08.367 --> 00:07:10.701
We're shooting it out in
New Orleans and Los Angeles.

00:07:10.701 --> 00:07:13.534
And she has this weekend
to approve,

00:07:13.534 --> 00:07:18.601
show up, do a wardrobe test,
and then show up on Monday.

00:07:18.601 --> 00:07:20.701
And she had scenes with me.

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:24.667
My character is going through
some devastating experiences.

00:07:24.667 --> 00:07:27.534
And so he's more reticent
on speaking.

00:07:27.534 --> 00:07:29.267
So it was all her.

00:07:29.267 --> 00:07:31.801
She had blocks and blocks
of dialogue.

00:07:31.801 --> 00:07:34.534
And I say things like...
"I don't know."

00:07:34.534 --> 00:07:36.501
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.501 --> 00:07:38.667
And then blocks and blocks
of dialogue.

00:07:38.667 --> 00:07:40.868
So I couldn't help her at all,
you know?

00:07:40.868 --> 00:07:44.033
And she was just magnificent.
-Yeah.

00:07:44.033 --> 00:07:46.367
-She just came in
and just crushed it.

00:07:46.367 --> 00:07:48.868
-Well, I'm glad
you were that convincing.

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:50.267
I'm glad you got her on board
to do it.

00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:52.801
-I kind of had to promise,
you know, certain things.

00:07:52.801 --> 00:07:54.400
I'd wash her car.
-Yeah, that's nice.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:55.834
You're that kind of actor.
-Take the trash out.

00:07:55.834 --> 00:07:57.767
Do whatever.
-You're that kind of guy.

00:07:57.767 --> 00:08:00.367
Here's what I'm concerned
and curious --

00:08:00.367 --> 00:08:02.501
is to how you got convinced
to do something.

00:08:02.501 --> 00:08:04.767
I have done the wonderful show
"Hot Ones,"

00:08:04.767 --> 00:08:06.534
which I love to watch.
-Oh, yes.

00:08:06.534 --> 00:08:08.067
-You also did "Hot Ones."
-Yeah.

00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:10.334
-And then you did "Hot Ones"
a second time.

00:08:10.334 --> 00:08:13.000
Now, I've known
a lot of people that do it.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.567
I like talking to people
who've done it.

00:08:14.567 --> 00:08:17.267
You're the first person
I've heard of who went back in.

00:08:17.267 --> 00:08:19.000
Because "Hot Ones" -- This is --

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.434
Well, I don't know
if you haven't seen it,

00:08:20.434 --> 00:08:22.868
but you eat chicken wings with
progressively hotter sauces.

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:24.868
It's the most uncomfortable
dining experience

00:08:24.868 --> 00:08:26.400
I've had in my life.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.634
-It's -- Well -- The answer
to your question, Seth,

00:08:30.634 --> 00:08:33.167
is I have no idea
why I did it a second time.

00:08:33.167 --> 00:08:34.968
-Yeah.
-I actually thought --

00:08:34.968 --> 00:08:36.267
Well, the first time I did it,

00:08:36.267 --> 00:08:38.968
I was promoting a movie
called "Why Him?"

00:08:38.968 --> 00:08:40.801
And I didn't know what it was.

00:08:40.801 --> 00:08:42.200
As soon as I was done,

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:43.801
I thought, "Well,
that'll never last.

00:08:43.801 --> 00:08:45.634
That's a silly premise."
-Yeah.

00:08:45.634 --> 00:08:50.300
But it was -- There's something
intriguing about that show.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.100
And so when they asked me to
come back, I said, "Oh, okay."

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.868
And then I thought,
"What am I doing?

00:08:54.868 --> 00:08:57.667
Oh, my God.
I'm doing that hot-sauce show!"

00:08:57.667 --> 00:09:01.367
And there was one hot sauce.
It was in a little jar.

00:09:01.367 --> 00:09:04.901
It didn't have as much of
an effect in my mouth

00:09:04.901 --> 00:09:07.267
as it did in the chemistry
of my body.

00:09:07.267 --> 00:09:10.000
-Yeah.
-I felt -- You ever have

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.934
one of those moments
where all of a sudden --

00:09:11.934 --> 00:09:14.334
"I'm overheating."
I went -- Whoof!

00:09:14.334 --> 00:09:17.033
And my whole head
was starting to sweat.

00:09:17.033 --> 00:09:20.267
And I was, like, wip--
It was unbelievable.

00:09:20.267 --> 00:09:22.334
I couldn't --
-It is a fascinating --

00:09:22.334 --> 00:09:24.300
I don't -- I mean, you know.
You do it.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.467
And you can't --
you stop thinking about

00:09:26.467 --> 00:09:28.334
what's the right or wrong thing
to say.

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:30.400
It's a really novel way
to do an interview

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.734
because at some point
I just kept talking

00:09:32.734 --> 00:09:35.434
because I thought if I stopped
talking I would die.

00:09:35.434 --> 00:09:38.767
-Yes. Yeah.
-And so it's -- I've never seen

00:09:38.767 --> 00:09:42.033
an interview with myself
where I have no memory of it.

00:09:42.033 --> 00:09:43.234
-And --
-But that's it.

00:09:43.234 --> 00:09:45.033
-Yeah.
-Do you remember what you said?

00:09:45.033 --> 00:09:47.400
-Almost nothing.
-What secrets did you reveal?

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.300
-I think it's too almost
too haunting to watch.

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:52.601
People are like,
"You should do ayahuasca."

00:09:52.601 --> 00:09:54.467
I'm like, "I think I did it."
-I think --

00:09:54.467 --> 00:09:55.834
-I think
there was maybe a little

00:09:55.834 --> 00:09:57.367
in some of the hot sauces.
-In some of the hot sauce.

00:09:57.367 --> 00:09:58.701
"I'm good."
-Yeah.

00:09:58.701 --> 00:10:00.000
Well, I'm very impressed
you did it twice.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.267
Thank you so much for being
